+## Q1 — May the stand-down DEADLINE and FORCE be owned by a launchd supervisor? — **AFFIRM (YES)**
+
+**R-9 clause relied on** (exhibit 11a:159-168, verbatim): "On an armed night the magistrate: (1) writes `night_plan.json` into the night's custody root and `docs/process/NIGHT_HANDBACK.md` …; (2) commits and pushes; (3) **ends the loop** (`ScheduleWakeup stop`) and the session. The standing turn-end live-work rule … is suspended on armed nights: the LaunchAgent is the wake source. Ed's only behavioural item: close T3 Code and any interactive `claude` before the armed hour. If he does not, the night REFUSES (R-3), records why, pushes the record, and the courier emails the refusal; nothing hangs."
+
+Reading: R-9 assigns the magistrate a cooperative act ("ends the loop … and the session") and names exactly one backstop for non-compliance — R-3's refusal. It states no deadline, reserves nothing to the magistrate exclusively, and forbids no machine actor. A supervisor that (i) requests the cooperative exit, (ii) enforces a deadline, and (iii) forces only the process group it launched adds a second layer beneath R-9's cooperative path; it does not convert R-9 into its opposite (charter §9): the magistrate still ends its own loop and session first, and R-3's refusal still stands behind everything. Ed's "close T3 Code and any interactive `claude`" residue is untouched because the proposal never kills an unowned process (exhibit 10 §1, "Kill only a process group the watchdog itself recorded; an unowned census hit is a hold, never a kill").
+
+**D-171 support** (exhibit 11b): item 7 fixes the margin as a number — "**Stand-down margin = 5 minutes before a window's t0** (30 was 'too much'); email at stand-down and at relaunch; Ed reads, does not reply" — a numeric margin presupposes a mechanical deadline; item 4 names the watchdog as an actor at stand-down — "it emails Ed at each launch and stand-down and honours a stop file". Both items were ratified after R-9 and speak to the same event; a supervisor-owned deadline is the only shape in the packet that can honour item 7's number regardless of what the session is doing.
+
+**Conditions I attach (severity in §9):**
+- C1-a (MATERIAL) The FORCE may reach only a process group the watchdog itself recorded at launch, validated by pid + process start time before signalling (exhibits 03:164, 09:166-169); never a census match. This is the D-161 line the seats drew and the packet adopted; it is also what keeps R-9's "Ed's only behavioural item" true.
+- C1-b (MATERIAL) The literal words of D-171 item 4 — "it emails Ed at each launch and stand-down" — cannot be met by the watchdog on the forced path: no repo file can send email (R-7, exhibit 11a:125-127) and all three seats concur (03 §8.2, 04 §8 item 1, 09 §8 item 2). The install notice to Ed must say so in plain words: cooperative stand-downs email themselves; a forced stand-down is reported by the next session's first email and by the night courier. The packet §1 already records this reading; it must reach Ed, not only the gate record.
+- C1-c (MATERIAL) With `--bg` as the spawn mechanism (Q2), the supervisor's "own process group" may not contain the session at all — `--bg` "return[s] immediately", which is what a daemonising launcher does, and the help is silent on where the session process lives. The Q2 bench proof must record the session's pid/pgid/sid relative to the launcher's, or the FORCE in this question has no target. Until that is recorded, Q1's YES is proven only for `-p` (whose child IS the session: `run_night.py:755-759` shape).
+- C1-d (NIT) R-9's sentence "the LaunchAgent is the wake source" was written for armed nights; under this design the LaunchAgent is the standing wake source. That rewording belongs in the same commit that installs the watchdog's decision-log entry (exhibit 03 §8.7 first bullet); it is bookkeeping, not a change of meaning.
