+## Q2 — `claude --bg` versus `claude -p` as the base; the bench proof — **REJECT the disposition as labeled ("`--bg` as base")**
+
+**What the evidence supports.** The only spawn shape with an executed, launchd-started, no-TTY proof in this packet is `-p`: the courier is `Popen(_courier_argv(...), cwd=REPO_ROOT, start_new_session=True)` (`run_night.py:755-759`), and its 09-02 run under the installed LaunchAgent delivered (`courier attempt=… sent=True`, exhibit 03:567-571 executed evidence; exhibit 04:110 cites the message id from the handback). `--bg` has no execution record anywhere in the packet: exhibit 10 §2 "Spawn" says "this magistrate session itself runs as a background job with `/loop` + `ScheduleWakeup` working … (Fable bench)" — that session was started from Terminal.app (exhibit 02:4; exhibit 03:23-25 shows the ancestry `zsh → claude → -zsh → login → Terminal`), i.e. WITH a TTY, by a human, which is not the case the watchdog needs. Exhibit 04 (the seat that read the `--bg` help) marks the launchd/no-TTY behaviour "[not executed]" (04:29) and recommends "ten bench minutes" before building around it (04:282). Charter §7: the proponent bears the burden; the packet's disposition rests on an unexecuted claim.
+
+**Help-text facts that bear on the choice (F-9).** `--permission-prompts` is defined "with --print"; `--output-format` "only works with --print". Under `--bg` the help does not say who answers a permission prompt; a background session that hits one with nobody attached is the "session hangs forever" row of every failure table. `-p` explicitly documents the no-TTY case ("when stdout is not a TTY") and the deny-on-prompt sink (`--permission-prompts none`). `--bg` does document `respawn` (binary updates) and `attach`/`logs` (human recovery) — real advantages, but advantages of an unproven path.
+
+**Ruling.** The build's base is whichever mechanism has an executed no-TTY launchd proof at install time. Today that is `-p`. `--bg` may be promoted to base only after the proof below is executed and its record placed in the gate custody; the supervisor's child argv must be a configuration value so the swap is one line (exhibits 03 §6 "every constant … injected", 04 §6 `--child-cmd`, 09 §6 injected spawning all agree on the shape). The packet's ordering (`--bg` base, `-p` fallback) is the reverse of what the evidence licenses; that is the disagreement with the labeled disposition.
+
+**Bench proof that must precede install** (all read-only with respect to repo and custody; temp roots only):
+
+1. Render a temporary user LaunchAgent (label `com.joulewise.bgproof`, `RunAtLoad true`, no `KeepAlive`, `StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath` under a temp dir, no `StartInterval`) whose `ProgramArguments` are a shell wrapper that runs, with stdin from `/dev/null` and no TTY: `/Users/edr/.local/bin/claude --bg --model <model> --permission-mode <mode the build will use> --allowedTools "Read,Bash,Write" "<prompt>"` where the prompt is: "First act: write `<tmp>/heartbeat` containing your pid, your process group id (`ps -o pgid= -p $$`), and `date +%s`. Then schedule a wakeup 3 minutes ahead with ScheduleWakeup and end this turn. After the wakeup, write `<tmp>/woke` with `date +%s`, then run `/usr/bin/pgrep -lf 'codex|claude|t3' > <tmp>/census.txt`, then end the loop with ScheduleWakeup stop:true."
+2. `launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) <plist>`; record the wrapper's stdout (the printed session id).
+3. Observables, each pasted into the record: (a) `<tmp>/heartbeat` appears within 300 s (D-127 §4 liveness shape); (b) `claude agents` lists the id as running AFTER the first turn ended — proves the session survives its first turn without a TTY; (c) `<tmp>/woke` appears ≈180 s after the heartbeat — proves `ScheduleWakeup` fires headless; (d) the heartbeat's pgid versus the wrapper's pgid (from the launchd log) — proves or disproves that a `killpg` on the launcher's group reaches the session (Q1 C1-c); (e) `claude logs <id>` shows no pending permission prompt; then a deliberate out-of-allowlist tool call in the prompt (e.g. `Edit` when only Read/Bash/Write are allowed) — record whether it is denied, executes, or hangs, which establishes the prompt sink under `--bg`; (f) `claude stop <id>` followed by `/usr/bin/pgrep -lf "codex|claude|t3"` — the census must show nothing from the proof; (g) `launchctl bootout` and delete the temp plist; `claude rm <id>`.
+4. Pass criterion: (a), (b), (c), (f) all true and (d) shows the session in the launcher's group (or the design switches the FORCE to `claude stop <id>` + census, recorded as such). Any failure → `-p` stays base; `--bg` is a follow-up row.
+
+Severity: MATERIAL (a design choice with an unproven premise on the critical path; not a blocker because the fallback is proven and one config line away).
